#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166542 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20b69474-34f6-5539-accf-5ebfc4f6eb0a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution With Inverse Hyperbolic Sine"}] :question/id #uuid "40513c35-cdde-57b5-be6e-0e19facd138e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8393" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{\\pi}{\\sqrt{2x^2+1}} \\, \\textrm{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^2+1}}\\,\\textrm{d}u = \\arsinh u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $2x^2 = \\left(\\sqrt{2}\\,x\\right)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u =\\sqrt{2}\\,x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\sqrt{2}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{2}\\,x)}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}\\text{\\arsinh}u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}\\text{\\arsinh}(\\sqrt{2}\\,x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166543 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "25d92a33-95e8-5860-b461-8291dac502d6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution With Inverse Hyperbolic Cosine"}] :question/id #uuid "34f929f2-63c4-5e9b-bd63-c42839a78ea6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8394" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{x^2-4}} \\,\\textrm{d}x   =$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nLet's rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{x^2-4}}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{3}{2} \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)^2-1}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic cosine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^2-1}}\\,\\textrm{d}u = \\arcosh u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u = \\dfrac x 2.$ Differentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= \\dfrac{1}{2}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x =2\\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =3\\text{\\arcosh}u+C \\\\  & =3\\text{\\arcosh}(\\frac{x}{2})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166544 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5aabe1c9-7a37-5b34-893f-9338f5c7c69a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution With Inverse Hyperbolic Tangent"}] :question/id #uuid "36f704ba-2263-5351-9a1e-b792b38481bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-8395" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{10}{1 - 4x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x   =$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-u^2} \\, \\textrm{d}u =\\artanh u +C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $4x^2 = \\left(2x\\right)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 2\\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{10}{1-4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\int \\frac{1}{1-(2x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =5\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =5\\text{\\artanh}u+C \\\\  & =5\\text{\\artanh}2x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $\\left|2x \\right| <    1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166545 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd13fafc-d765-5e44-aa5a-79c2fff71212" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution With Inverse Hyperbolic Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "b2d41cac-2c65-5d2d-b847-0c1f9bfdf48a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8396" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nEvaluate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2 - 4}}\\, \\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nNote that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2 - 4}}\\, \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac {x}{2} \\right)^2 - 1}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse hyperbolic cosine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^2-1}}\\, \\textrm{d}u = \\arcosh{u}+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $\\left(\\dfrac {x}{2} \\right)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u = \\dfrac {x}{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x = 2\\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $4$ | $2\\sqrt{5}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $\\sqrt{5}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2 - 4}}\\, \\textrm{d}x &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac {x}{2} \\right)^2 - 1}} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\[5pt]\n\n&= \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{2}^{\\sqrt 5} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{u^2 - 1}} \\cdot (2 \\, \\textrm{d}u) \\[5pt]\n\n&= \\int_{2}^{\\sqrt 5} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\sqrt{u^2 - 1}} \\[5pt]\n\n&= \\arcosh u \\Big|_{2}^{\\sqrt 5} \\[5pt]\n\n&= \\arcosh{\\sqrt 5} - \\arcosh{2} \\[5pt]\n\n&=  \\ln\\left(\\sqrt 5 + \\sqrt{\\left(\\sqrt 5\\right)^2 - 1}\\right) - \\ln\\left(2 + \\sqrt{\\left(2\\right)^2 - 1}\\right)  \\[5pt]\n\n&= \\ln\\left(2 + \\sqrt{5}\\right) -  \\ln\\left(2 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)  \\[5pt]\n\n& = \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{2 + \\sqrt{5}}{2 + \\sqrt{3}}  \\right)\n\n}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339392 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd13fafc-d765-5e44-aa5a-79c2fff71212" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution With Inverse Hyperbolic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b9106a2-a41c-5286-925e-b27c3bdbc8d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b9106a2-a41c-5286-925e-b27c3bdbc8d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339394} :answer-field/id #uuid "cecfdc2c-0da0-5a50-a0b8-92dc7f045bce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339393} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c08e2de-7356-5e0e-aae9-74476960a431" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c08e2de-7356-5e0e-aae9-74476960a431" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339396} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cf46f78-25b4-5dc1-ae89-06193e4318c4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339395}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4834efb9-b435-5d43-9063-edbca8c7e7ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-346519" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{20}{25-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$artanh\\,(${{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\nNote that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{20}{25-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C,\n$$\n\nthe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{5}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=5\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{20}{25-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{3/5}\\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{3/5}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{3/5} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})-4\\operatorname{artanh}⁡0 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339397 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd13fafc-d765-5e44-aa5a-79c2fff71212" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution With Inverse Hyperbolic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6bdd88d-8bcd-57b6-8c7a-1ed153c9aa4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339399} {:answer/id #uuid "51de2cad-8271-5428-82b1-e4217bff8f6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51de2cad-8271-5428-82b1-e4217bff8f6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339400} :answer-field/id #uuid "31faa681-e4b4-53fb-80d8-39d3018ae65b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339398} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "445853b9-3323-5389-b5d6-37e44ea5324e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "445853b9-3323-5389-b5d6-37e44ea5324e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339402} :answer-field/id #uuid "89331699-d334-5016-958d-0b3ef7054104" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339401}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0802b7c5-9d48-5121-89e7-6efe6d0dee84" :question/math-academy-id "q-350220" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{40}{64-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}\n$artanh\\,(${{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\nNote that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{40}{64-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{5}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C,\n$$\n\nthe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{8})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{8}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{8}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=8\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{40}{64-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{3/4}\\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot 8\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{3/4}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{3/4} \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})-5\\operatorname{artanh}⁡0 \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167295 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "25d92a33-95e8-5860-b461-8291dac502d6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution With Inverse Hyperbolic Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f953e3a-f202-578a-90a7-7e4516afb7f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339404} {:answer/id #uuid "6d199c02-d18e-57db-9851-63dbd1a166bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339405} {:answer/id #uuid "665cbabf-3197-51b8-a77f-7646847d9496" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339406} {:answer/id #uuid "4e76b3e6-93f6-5187-a538-b2844161d7d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339407} {:answer/id #uuid "d511bc7d-00b3-5dd0-8e53-44b381f00a25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339408}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d199c02-d18e-57db-9851-63dbd1a166bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339405} :answer-field/id #uuid "49e4b91b-4dca-5f59-b383-9ca1bc53b962" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1722041-c374-58c7-a9f0-5a7400016292" :question/math-academy-id "q-95432" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic cosine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $16{x}^{2}=(4x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=4x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(4x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡4x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339409 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20b69474-34f6-5539-accf-5ebfc4f6eb0a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution With Inverse Hyperbolic Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da76dea3-8043-5815-b23a-d2fc1dc6d25d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339411} {:answer/id #uuid "b27d105b-0874-5daa-8275-9a454da49318" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339412} {:answer/id #uuid "27f0e22a-1b3c-5f74-931b-10b63ae08068" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339413} {:answer/id #uuid "7fc7f8b5-d2cd-5a5a-b551-261c313e788a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339414} {:answer/id #uuid "a02bd2dc-9365-58a3-b472-900d28c13553" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a02bd2dc-9365-58a3-b472-900d28c13553" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339415} :answer-field/id #uuid "c21aa344-5a21-5a0d-942a-73cec2cee122" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339410}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2ed400b6-a430-53b9-bebe-b5523b7a7554" :question/math-academy-id "q-95690" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{{e}^{x}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2x}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${e}^{2x}={({e}^{x})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u={e}^{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{e}^{x}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2x}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{{e}^{x}}{\\sqrt{{({e}^{x})}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{u}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡{e}^{x}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339416 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20b69474-34f6-5539-accf-5ebfc4f6eb0a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution With Inverse Hyperbolic Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "774aa225-34df-51f0-9119-01e4772c8e31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\sqrt{7}\\,x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339418} {:answer/id #uuid "7c998079-0ccf-5d38-ad43-c6503efa6137" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\sqrt{7}\\,x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339419} {:answer/id #uuid "2adf096f-57f5-522b-9833-e03d975a2ee1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\sqrt{7}\\,x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339420} {:answer/id #uuid "7f1e9266-5072-59d6-814f-3e713f2e875a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\sqrt{7}\\,x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339421} {:answer/id #uuid "c4fedcc8-33b0-5f4d-97f9-af0e5e4a6a48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\sqrt{7}\\,x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "774aa225-34df-51f0-9119-01e4772c8e31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\sqrt{7}\\,x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339418} :answer-field/id #uuid "284e6169-d92e-5d65-99df-0decf3cd4ded" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339417}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8b27c48f-3420-52ec-834f-0ff83c3b89b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-95692" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{7{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $7{x}^{2}={(\\sqrt{7}\\,x)}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\sqrt{7}\\,x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{7}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{7{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{7}\\,x)}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\sqrt{7}\\,x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339423 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20b69474-34f6-5539-accf-5ebfc4f6eb0a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution With Inverse Hyperbolic Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "994bee9d-c0b5-515b-b462-199937bfd78b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡{e}^{2x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339425} {:answer/id #uuid "0dc9a866-1b8d-5814-9